# Metal-organic frameworks-derived CoP anchored on MXene toward an efficient bifunctional electrode with enhanced lithium storage

a Key Laboratory of Polar Materials and Devices (MOE), Department of Electronics, East China Normal University, Shanghai, 200241, China b Collaborative Innovation Center of Extreme Optics, Shanxi University, Taiyuan, Shanxi 030006, China
## A R T I C L E  I N F O
## A B S T R A C T
Keywords:   
MOF   
Cobalt phosphide   
MXene   
Bifunctional electrodes   
Lithium-ion batteries   
Lithium-sulfur batteries
It is significant to exploit novel electrode materials because at present the severe capacity decline and slow ion kinetics limit the broad application of lithium-based batteries. Herein, we have prepared the metal–organic frameworks-derived CoP anchored on $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}$ MXene (MOFs-CoP $@$ MXene) and fully explored its synergy as an efficient bifunctional electrode for enhanced lithium storage for the first time. It is gratifying that the prepared MOFs-CoP $@$ MXene provides a high capacity of $706.5\ \mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}}$ after 200 cycles at $0.2\mathrm{~A~g~}^{-1}$ , as the anode for lithium-ion batteries (LIBs). It can provide 585.8 mAh ${\mathbf g}^{-1}$ after 1000 cycles with $0.5\mathrm{~A~g~}^{-1}$ . The MOFs$\mathsf{C o P}@$ MXene anode has robust lithium-ion storage capacity, indicating that it can increase the charge transfer rate and improve $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ diffusion kinetics with enhanced electrochemical activity. As lithium-sulfur batteries (LSBs) cathode, MOFs-CoP $@$ MXene/S shows $796.9\ \mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}}$ after 300 cycles at 0.2C. MOFs-derived CoP coupling with MXene exhibits enhanced capture capacity of sulfur, facilitating the conversion of polysulfides. The results show that the MOFs-CoP $@$ MXene composite prepared for the first time are feasible as both the anode of LIBs and the cathode of LSBs.
## 1. Introduction
In recent years, the large-scale application of new energy vehicles has put forward higher requirements for the development of highperformance batteries [1,2]. Therefore, researchers have paid exten­ sive attention to the cathode and anode of lithium-based batteries, which is the key to improving lithium-based batteries’ specific capacity. Transition metal phosphides have been widely used towards anodes of LIBs with high electrical conductivity, large theoretical specific capacity and fast reaction kinetics [3]. Cobalt phosphide (CoP) is an ideal ma­ terial for improving lithium storage [4,5]. According to reports, the metal-organic frameworks abbreviated as MOFs, consist of organic li­ gands and metal ions, which can derive many metal compounds, such as oxides, sulfides and phosphides [6]. These MOF-derived metal com­ pounds have been used as lithium-ion batteries with high theoretical activity and large specific surface area [7,8]. However, MOF-derived phosphides still have colossal research potential. ZIF-67 is a kind of Co-MOFs with a large amount of carbon and a three-dimensional (3D) topological structure [9,10]. The disadvantages of ZIF-67-derived CoP are apparent, high surface energy, severe volume expansion, complicated synthesis routes and difficult microstructure control [11]. Therefore, the innovative preparation of cobalt phosphide derived from ZIF-67 with good stability is of great significance. It is reported that MXene can be used to enhance active materials to improve their resil­ ience in charge/discharge cycles [12]. MXenes, 2D transition metal carbides, have been extensively studied in lithium-based batteries since their discovery in 2011 [13–15]. $\mathrm{Ti_{3}C_{2}T_{x}}$ $\mathrm{\DeltaT_{X}}$ means -O-, -OH and/or -F) is a member of the material family of MXenes [16,17]. Because of its stable 2D structure, hydrophilic surface and low lithium-ion diffusion energy barrier, $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}$ MXene is an ideal substrate for lithium-ion storage [18,19].
Cathode materials have a more significant influence on the energy density of LIBs compared with anode materials. Sulfur is regarded as a promising cathode material, but there are still considerable challenges in capacity reduction and low Coulombic efficiency [20]. There is poor conductivity, slow redox kinetics, volume expansion of sulfur molecules and shuttle effect of lithium polysulfides (LiPS) [21]. Although chemical adsorption and physical encapsulation can inhibit the volume change of sulfur molecules, it is challenging to prevent LiPS from reaching the anode from the cathode through the separator. It is reported that some transition metals (Pt, Ni and Co, etc.) can improve the kinetics of pol­ ysulfides and block the shuttle effect [22]. ZIF-67 has a large pore vol­ ume, which is conducive to the adsorption of LiPS, and the electrochemical catalysis of CoP can improve the kinetics of LSBs [23]. MOFs-derived CoP can play a synergistic role in adsorption and catal­ ysis. MOFs-derived CoP coupling with MXenes can act as a binder to connect individual particles, making electron transfer more efficient, thus further improving charge transfer dynamics and structural stability [24–26]. The MOFs-CoP $@$ MXene composites can avoid the aggregation of $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathsf{S}_{\mathrm{x}}$ $2\leq\mathbf{x}\leq6)$ and have great potential in LSBs in cycle stability and storage capacity [27].
Herein, in order to determine the potential of MOFs-CoP@MXene composites in lithium-based batteries, ZIF-67 and MXene nanosheets are self-assembled and converted into MOFs-CoP@MXene in situ through a low-temperature phosphating process. MOFs-CoP nano­ structures are anchored on the MXene network. It is worth noting that the MOFs-CoP $@$ MXene hybrid has unique dual-functions that can enhance lithium storage. We also discuss the reaction mechanism of MOFs-CoP $@$ MXene applied to LIBs anode and the inhibition of the shuttle effect of $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\boldsymbol{S}_{\mathrm{x}}$ when used to LSBs cathode. MOFs-derived CoP not only has the unique 3D structure and specific surface area of MOFs but also has the catalytic effect of cobalt phosphide on the reaction kinetics of lithium-ions. Besides, the $\mathrm{Ti_{3}C_{2}T_{x}}$ MXene nanosheets with 2D layered structure and wealthy terminal functional groups play the role of sup­ porting substrate and have pseudocapacitive properties. The structural engineering of assembling ZIF-67 $@$ MXene and phosphating to prepare MOFs-derived CoP@MXene is reported for the first time. The MOFsCoP $@$ MXene composite with a unique design towards a dualfunctional electrode can shed some light on electrochemical energy storage [28,29].
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Materials
The MAX powder $\mathrm{\Ti}_{3}\mathrm{AlC}_{2}$ , $98\%$ , 500 mesh) was purchased from Kai Kai Laizhou Co., Ltd. Lithium fluoride (LiF, $99.99\%$ ) was purchased from J&K Scientific. Hydrofluoric acid (HF, $\geq40\%$ ), hydrochloric acid (HCl, $38.0\%$ ) and methanol $\mathrm{\cdot{CH_{3}O H}}$ , $\geq99.7\%)$ ) were purchased from Sino­ pharm Co., Ltd. Cobalt nitrate hexahydrate $\mathrm{(CoNO_{3}{\cdot}6H_{2}O}$ , $99\%$ , AR) and sodium hypophosphite monohydrate $(\mathrm{NaH_{2}P O_{2}}{\cdot}\mathrm{H_{2}O}$ , $\geq85\%$ ) were purchased from Aladdin. Co., Ltd. 2-methylimidazole (mIM) was pur­ chased from Adamas-beta. N-methyl pyrrolidone (NMP) and Poly­ vinylidene fluoride (PVDF, hsv900) were purchased from SigmaAldrich. The resistivity of the industrial distilled water used is about $18\mathrm{M}\Omega$ .
2.2. Preparation of the ZIF-67@MXene composites.
In order to prepare ZIF-67 $@$ MXene composites, we prepared MXene at first, and then ZIF-67 was self-assembled in a methanol solution containing MXene. Specifically, $_{4\mathrm{~g~}}$ LiF and $2\ \mathrm{g\Ti_{3}A l C_{2}}$ powder were sequentially added to $80~\mathrm{mL}$ of 8 M HCl for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ at $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The mixture was then ultrasonicated in a plastic centrifuge tube filled with ${\bf N}_{2}$ for $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ . The $\mathrm{Ti_{3}C_{2}T_{x}}$ MXene powder was prepared by washing with water and centrifugation three times until the pH reached approximately 7, and then drying in a freeze dryer. Dissolve $\boldsymbol{0.984}\ \mathrm{g}$ of 2-methylimidazole in $75~\mathrm{mL}$ of methanol and $0.747\textrm{g C o}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ in another $75~\mathrm{mL}$ methanol, and slowly pour the ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ salt solution into the former with vigorous stirring. $1.558\ {\mathrm{g}}{}$ MXene was subsequently dispersed in a so­ lution in methanol. ZIF-67 $@$ MXene was obtained after aging at room temperature for $24\mathrm{h}$ with methanol.
2.3. Preparation of the MOFs-CoP@MXene composites.
Firstly, ZIF-67@MXene was heated to $400^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 1 h in a tube furnace in the flowing of Ar gas to remove the organic substance. Secondly, the sample was phosphate with sodium hypophosphite $\mathrm{\langle(NaH_{2}P O_{2}{\cdot}H_{2}O)}$ . During the phosphation, place the appropriate amount of the carbonized ZIF-67@MXene and $\mathrm{NaH_{2}P O_{2}\cdot H_{2}O}$ on two separate quartz boats with $\mathrm{NaH_{2}P O_{2}{\cdot}H_{2}O}$ on the upstream side. Finally, the MOFs-CoP $@$ MXene composite can be synthesized after heating at $350^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{3\mathrm{h}}$ . For com­ parison, metal–organic frameworks-derived CoP was obtained with the same steps except adding MXene. The MOFs-CoP $@$ MXene/S powder was made by ball milling with the mass ratio of sulfur about $70\%$ and annealing at tube furnace at $175^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under the Ar atmosphere for $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ .
## 2.4. Characterization
Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) and transmission electron mi­ croscopy (TEM) were obtained to analyze the prepared samples using Gemini 450 (Zeiss, Germany) and JEM 2100F (JEOL, Japan), respec­ tively. X-ray diffraction (XRD) and X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) were obtained using D8 ADVANCE with $\mathrm{Cu}\ K\upalpha$ radiation (Bruker, Netherlands) and Axis ULTRA (Kratos, USA) at $15~\mathrm{kV}$ , respectively. Fourier transform infrared (FTIR) was obtained to analyze chemical bonds by Nicolet iS50 (Thermo Fisher, USA). Atomic force microscope (AFM, Bruker MultiMode 8, Netherlands) was used to characterize the thickness of the $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}$ MXene nanosheets. The pore volume and specific surface area were obtained by a gas sorption analyzer (Autosorb-IQ3, China). Thermo Gravimetric Analyzer (TGA/SDTA851e, Switzerland) was used to analyze the proportion of each component.
## 2.5. Electrochemical measurements
Both LIBs and LSBs use a typical CR2032 coin-type cell for electro­ chemical testing. In the case of LIB anodes, the prepared MOFsCoP@MXene, carbon and PVDF $\mathrm{(m/m/m=8{:}1{:}1)}$ were prepared into a slurry with NMP solvent, and the slurry was all coated and dried for 13 h at $110^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The active materials on the copper foil are controlled within $1.4{-}1.9\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ . $1\mathrm{MLiPF}_{6}$ was used as the electrolyte, with the solvent including DEC, DMC and EC (1:1:1, v/v/v) [30]. Both the reference electrode and counter electrode were using Li foil. Celgard 2400 was utilized as separators of LIBs. As for the LSB cathode, a slurry containing MOFs-CoP $@$ MXene/S was applied to the aluminum foil with a sulfur loading ranging from 1.6 to $2.0\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ . $30~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ electrolyte for per Li-S battery contains $\mathrm{LiNO}_{3}$ (0.2 M) and LiTFSI (1 M) solutes including DME and DOL (1:1, $\mathbf{v}/\mathbf{v}$ ) solvents [31]. Both LIBs and LSBs were assembled in Mikrouna Super $2440/750/900$ . The charge/discharge cycles of the batteries were examined using CT3001A (LANHE, Wuhan). The CV curves and Nyquist plots were obtained using CHI660E (Chenhua, Shanghai).
## 3. Results and discussion
First of all, the synthetic illustration of the MOFs-CoP $@$ MXene composites was shown in Fig. 1. First, the $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{AlC}_{2}$ MAX precursor was engaged in the LiF $+\ \mathrm{HCl}$ solution for $^{24\mathrm{~h~}}$ to prepare $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}$ MXene nanosheets. Subsequently, the MXene nanosheets were engaged in a methanol solution of 2-methylimidazole and ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ . ZIF-67 grew homo­ geneously and anchored on MXene because of the complexation of co­ balt ions and the adsorption of surface oxygen functional groups on MXene. After the treatments of carbonization and phosphation. MOFsCoP@MXene composite can be obtained. Atomic diagrams of ZIF-67 and $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ are shown in Fig. S1. Herein, we have developed the met­ al–organic frameworks derived CoP anchored on MXene as LIB anode and LSB cathode for the first time.
Fig. 2a and b are ZIF-67-derived CoP and $\mathrm{Ti_{3}C_{2}T_{x}}$ MXene composites described by the scanning electron microscope (SEM). Metal-organic frameworks-derived CoP nanoparticles are uniform in morphology, showing a rhombohedral shape with a side length of about $600\ \mathrm{nm}$ . MXene is in the form of 2D flakes, covering the surface of the MOFs-CoP nanoparticles. Fig. S2 shows that MOFs-CoP nanoparticles can grow in the interlamination of MXene nanosheets successfully. As shown in the MOFs-CoP $@$ MXene structural representation of Fig. 1, the novel sandwich-like structure can be prepared. Fig. S3a shows that multi-layer $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}$ has micron-scale layer spacing. The typical (0 0 2) plane of $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}$ MXene can be related to the distance of $1.32\:\mathrm{nm}$ , as shown in Fig. S3b. Fig. S3c of the AFM image shows that the few-layer $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}$ MXene nanosheets has approximately $10\ \mathrm{nm}$ of thickness. The Raman spectra (Fig. S3d) shows that the peaks at 617, 264 and $430\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ correspond to in-plane vibrations $\mathrm{(E_{g})}$ of Ti, C and the oxygen-containing functional groups. The height of $156~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ corresponds to the Ti-atom’s out-ofplane symmetric-vibration $(\mathbf{A}_{1\mathrm{g}})$ [32]. Fig. 2c, d depict the hetero­ structure of MOFs-CoP is a hollow polyhedral structure with curved edges. MXene can protect the MOFs-CoP and MXene nanosheets by TEM.
It is clear that the MOFs-CoP are easily deformed and improves the stability of the MOFs-CoP $@$ MXene by forming a heterojunction. The inset of Fig. 2d shows that the SAED pattern matches the standard electron diffraction ring of cobalt phosphide very well. There is an HRTEM of the MOFs-CoP@MXene composites shown in Fig. 2e. The region of MXene is on the left, with the $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}$ MXene’s typical (002) plane. The right-hand section represents MOFs-CoP, which shows clear lattice fringes in Fig. 2f. There are two crystal planes with an angle of $60^{\circ}$ corresponding to the (00 1) (d-spacing of $0.43\mathrm{nm}\dot{}$ ) and (101) (d-spacing of $0.41\mathrm{nm}\dot{}$ ) planes of CoP derived from ZIF-67, respectively. The HAADF and element mapping in STEM mode are shown in Fig. 2g and $\mathbf{h-k},$ respectively. There is a nanosheet of MXene covering the top of two nanoparticles of MOFs-CoP (Fig. 2g), suggesting MOFs-CoP nano­ particles coupling with MXene successfully. The elemental mapping can distinguish the distribution relationship between MXene and MOFs-CoP. Ti, Co, P and C elements are also reflected in Fig. 2h-k, respectively. Figs. S4a, b shows the change from ZIF-67 to MOFs-derived CoP after phosphating [33]. Fig. S4c is the image of SEM-EDX, indicating the number of P atoms is larger than that of Co atoms. Table S1 shows the atomic ratio of P and Co according to the area of peaks, exhibiting that the number of Co atoms is larger than that of P atoms. These are because the concentration of phosphorus on the surface could be higher than that on the inside. Fig. S5 shows the Raman spectra of ZIF-67 before and after phosphation, where 177 and $255~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ correspond to the vibration modes of N-Co-N and 519 and $682~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ correspond to the stretching of mIM molecules’ $\mathsf{C}\mathrm{-CH_{3}}$ , which is consistent with previous work [34]. The new Raman peaks at 233 and $471\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ of MOFs-derived CoP could be related to the Co-P deformation.
XRD patterns of MOFs-CoP, $\mathrm{Ti_{3}C_{2}T_{x}}$ MXene and MOFs-CoP $@$ MXene are shown in Fig. 3a. The peak near $7.8^{\circ}$ corresponds to MXene’s (002) plane, and the peak near $16.1^{\circ}$ corresponds to its (0 0 4) plane, indicating that there is no phase of the $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{AlC}_{2}$ precursor and the phase of $\mathrm{Ti_{3}C_{2}T_{x}}$ MXene is formed [35]. Cobalt-containing ZIF-67 has been derived as cobalt phosphide during the phosphating process because the diffraction peaks of both MOFs-CoP and MOFs-CoP@MXene are consistent with JCPDS No. 29-0497, the orthorhombic phase of CoP. Fig. 3b shows the FTIR spectrum of $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}$ MXene, ZIF-67, MOFs-CoP and the MOFsCOP@MXene. There are five peaks in a range of 757 to $1424~\mathrm{{cm}}^{-1}$ , indicating the imidazole ring’s stretching and bending modes because precursor ZIF-67 contains the raw material of 2-methylimidazole. As for the MOFs-CoP, there is one peak at $1602\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ which matches the $\scriptstyle\mathtt{P=O}$ bonds’ stretching and two peaks located at 1142 and $1242\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ indexed to the stretching of P–O. The frequency peaks at 652, 1110 and 3432 $\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ are allocated to the expansion and contraction of Ti-O, the stretching of $\scriptstyle\mathrm{O-H}$ and –OH, respectively. Fig. S6 verified the existence of Ti, Co, C, N and P elements. Fig. 3c–f show the HR-XPS results of C 1s, P 2p, Ti 2p and Co 2p, respectively. The peaks at 287.3, 286.5, 285.6 and $284.5\mathrm{eV}$ can be matched with the $\scriptstyle0-\mathbf{C}=0$ , C–F, C–O–C and C–C, as shown in Fig. 3c. The C-Ti- ${\bf\nabla}\cdot{\bf O_{x}}$ bond is assigned to 281.7 eV peak. P–O, P $\mathsf{2p}_{1/2}$ and $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{P}}2\boldsymbol{\mathrm{p}}_{3/2}$ of the MOFs-derived CoP can be assigned to the three peaks located at 133.2, 129.7 and 127.7 eV [36], as shown in Fig. 3d. Fig. 3e shows that the Ti $\mathsf{2p}_{1/2}$ and Ti $\mathsf{2p}_{3/2}$ for Ti-C in Ti 2p spectrum correspond to the peaks at 458.4 and $454.6\mathrm{eV}$ . Similarly, Ti $\mathsf{2p}_{1/2}$ of Ti
O correspond to the peaks located at $463.0\mathrm{eV}$ and Ti $\mathsf{2p}_{1/2}$ of Ti- ${\bf\nabla}\cdot{\bf O_{x}}$ can be assigned to the peaks at 460.8 eV [37]. MXene with oxygencontaining functional groups can improve the properties of pseudoca­ pacitance of lithium-based batteries [38]. Fig. 3f shows that the peaks at $796.9\mathrm{eV}$ and 781.2 are deconvoluted into Co (III) $\mathsf{2p}_{1/2}$ and Co (III) $\mathsf{2p3/}$ 2 [22,39]. Furthermore, there are two satellite peaks at 803.2 and 876.5 eV. The inner mechanism of MOFs-CoP nanoparticles anchored on MXene nanosheets can be explained from chemical bonding and phys­ ical adsorption. Ti-O-P-Co chemical bond could be formed between MOFs-CoP with active phosphorus element and $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}$ MXene with oxygen-containing functional group. MOFs-CoP nanoparticles and MXene nanosheets can be combined physical adsorption such as van der Waals force. In addition, to define the content of the active material for the LIBs anodes and LSBs cathodes, we conducted thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) on MXene, MOFs-CoP $@$ MXene under air atmosphere, MOFs-CoP $@$ MXene and MOFs-CoP $@$ MXene/S under $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ atmosphere. MXene can be oxidized to $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ at around $350~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ . MOFs-CoP can be oxidized to $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ at $400~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and completely oxidized to $\mathrm{Co}_{3}(\mathrm{PO}_{4})_{2}$ at $500~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ [40]. XRD of the reaction products of MOFs-CoP at 400 and
$500^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ are shown in Fig. S7. As the LIB anode, the content of MOFs-CoP in MOFs-CoP $@$ MXene is about $43.2\%$ (Fig. S8). As the LSB cathode, the content of sulfur in MOFs-CoP $@$ MXene/S is about $62.7\%$ (Fig. S9), based on that sulfur can sublime at $200^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ . Fig. S10 shows that MOFsCoP@MXene composites possess large specific surface areas of $202~\mathrm{{m}}^{2}$ ${\bf g}^{-1}$ by $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ sorption analyzer based on the Brunauer-Emmer-Teller (BET) model.
We studied the electrochemical performance of MOFs-CoP $@$ MXene composite material as the anode of LIBs. The first three CV curves of MOFs-CoP $@$ MXene electrodes between 0.01 and $3\mathrm{~V~}$ was displayed in Fig. $\mathtt{4a}$ , with $0.2\:\mathrm{mV}\:s^{-1}$ . The redox peaks in the initial lithiation process were very different from the subsequent second and third CV curves. There could be reversible $\mathbf{Li_{x}C o P}$ formed during lithium intercalation of MOFs-derived CoP with the broad peak near $1.4{\mathrm{V}}.$ Li-ion inserting the MXene interlayer corresponds to the peak at around $0.75\mathrm{~V~}$ . The continuous wide slop below $_{1\mathrm{~V~}}$ can be attributed to the continuous transformation of Co and $\mathrm{Li}_{3}\mathrm{P}_{\it\cdot}$ , as well as irreversible reactions per­ taining to the SEI membrane. There is the formation for the MOFsderived CoP at the anodic peak of $1.1\mathrm{~V~}$ during the first oxidation cycle. The reversible reaction of the MOFs-CoP corresponds to $1.05\mathrm{V}$ of the anodic peak. After the first cycle, the battery’s cathodic peaks move to $0.52\mathrm{V}$ in the subsequent cycles. It is reproducible and stable of the CV curves, indicating that the MOFs-CoP $@$ MXene composite has good sta­ bility. Here are reactions (Eqs. (1)-(3)) between MOFs-CoP $@$ MXene and $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ [19,41–44]:
$$
\begin{array}{r l}&{\mathrm{CoP}+\mathrm{x}L i^{+}+x e^{-}\leftrightarrow L i_{x}C o P}\\ &{L i_{x}C o P+(3-x)L i^{+}+(3-x)e^{-}\leftrightarrow L i_{3}P+C o}\\ &{T i_{3}C_{2}T_{x}(T=O,O H,F)+\mathrm{x}L i^{+}+x e^{-}\leftrightarrow T i_{3}C_{2}T_{x}L i_{x}}\end{array}
$$
Fig. 4b displays that the charge/discharge plots for MOFsCoP@MXene at $200\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{\g}^{-1}$ have a charge–discharge capacity of 873.9 and $644.7\ \mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}}$ , respectively, in the initial cycle. We then got the Coulombic efficiency (CE) of $69.6\%$ in the first cycle, which can be resulted in the irreversible product formed by MOFs-CoP and MXene reacts with the SEI membrane. The SEI membrane formed in the first cycle would irreversibly consume lithium ions because of the solid­ –liquid interface between the anodes and the electrolyte. In addition, the polyhedral structure and high specific surface area can also lead to lower initial Coulombic efficiency. In the second cycle, the CE of the MOFs$\mathsf{C o P}@$ MXene electrode reached $90\%$ . Furthermore, in the next cycles, it is satisfying that the CE remained above $96\%$ (Fig. 4b and d). The reversible capacities of the MOFs-CoP $@$ MXene anodes are as high as 818.6, 655.9, 594.1, 450.0, 328.4 and $219.1\ \mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}}$ at 100, 200, 500, 1000, 2000 and $5000\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively. The rate performance of MOFs-CoP $@$ MXene was shown in Fig. 4c. The MOFs-CoP $@$ MXene electrode has a reversible capacity that can return to about 716.7 mAh ${\tt g}^{-1}$ with $100\mathrm{mAg}^{-1}$ in subsequent cycles. The MOFs-CoP $@$ MXene electrode demonstrated excellent cycle stability and capacity retention. Similarly, at the current density of 0.1, 0.2, 0.5, 1, 2 and $5\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}}$ , the MOFs-CoP anodes have the capacities of 541.1, 336.6, 211.9, 94.4, 44.8 and 10.9 mAh $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ , respectively. There is a low electrodes’ capacity retention at high current densities without using MXene. The pure MXene electrode has good stability despite its low capacity at $1\mathrm{~A~g~}^{-1}$ (Fig. S11). It further confirms that the synergistic effect of the MOFsCoP@MXene composites improves the lithium storage capacity.
Fig. 4d shows that the MOFs-CoP $@$ MXene electrodes exhibit good cycling performance. There is an excellent reversible capacity with 200 mA ${\bf g}^{-1}$ of $706.5\ \mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}}$ after 200 cycles, while MOFs-CoP has only $335.4\ \mathrm{mAh}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . The MOFs-CoP $@$ MXene anodes have a very stable ca­ pacity even at higher current densities. Fig. 4e shows that the MOFsCoP@MXene anodes reach the lowest 503.4 mAh $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ in the 37th cycle at the current density of $0.5\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ . Because of the phase transfer of MOFs-CoP, the specific capacity of the LIBs continued to increase in the subsequent cycles, maintaining an enhanced capacity $(585.8\ \mathrm{mAh\g^{-1})}$ at 1000 cycles. This kind of conversion electrode material with increased capacity caused by the activation process has been widely reported. Due to the lithiation-delithiation, MOFs-CoP could be reasonably destroyed, resulting in increasing the active sites of lithium storage [40].
To gain insight into the charge storage kinetics for MOFsCoP@MXene electrodes, we performed CV curves in Fig. 5a between 0.2 and $10\mathrm{mV}s^{-1}$ at different current density. Diffusion contributions caused by the lithiation process and the charge transfer in the surface are the two charge storage mechanisms of LIB cycles. The power-law equation can determine the charge storage transfer of typical LIBs [45,46].
$$
\begin{array}{l}{{i={\bf{a}}\nu^{b}}}\\ {{\phantom{{\displaystyle\ l o g i=}}}}\\ {{\log i={\bf{b l o g}}\nu+\log a}}\end{array}
$$
where $\nu$ represents the scan rate and i indicates the current. The fitting curves of the v-log i are shown in Fig. S12, according to the deformation of Eq. (4). Fig. 5b shows that the b-value is obtained by the slope of Eq. (5). Generally, the b-value $=1$ indicates that the capacitance is the main factor during the cycle, and ${\bf b}=0.5$ indicates that diffusion control is dominant. The ${\mathbf{{Li}}^{+}}$ storage kinetics of MOFs-CoP $@$ MXene anodes in­ cludes both diffusion-controlled and capacitive contributions, according to the b-values in Fig. 5b between 0.69 and 0.96. Further, to quantita­ tively distinguish a detailed rate of the capacitive contribution and diffusion-control, we calculated by Eq. (6) [46,47]:
$$
i(\mathbf{V})=k_{1}\nu+k_{2}\nu^{1/2}
$$
Fig. 5c shows that the contribution ratio of the capacitance increases with the scan rate increases. Fig. 5d shows the capacitive contribution ratio of the MOFs-CoP $@$ MXene electrode is $69.1\%$ at $5{\mathrm{~mV~}}s^{-1}$ . The MOFs-CoP $@$ MXene electrodes have excellent cycle stability and rate capability, which is ascribe to synergistic effects between MXene with pseudo-capacitance and MOFs-derived CoP with high conductivity, which can promote electron/proton transport and shorten the diffusion path of lithium ions.
The Nyquist diagrams include both high-frequency semi-circles and low-frequency oblique straight lines. Fig. 6a shows the EIS spectra of the MOFs-CoP, MXene and MOFs-CoP $@$ MXene. The linear slope in the re­ gion of low-frequency is related to the Warburg resistance $(\mathsf{W}_{0})$ . The intercept between the $z'$ axis and the semi-circles in the region of highfrequency can reflect the resistance in the electrolyte $(\mathbb{R}_{\Omega})$ . The semicircle’s diameter is regarded as the charge transfer resistance $\mathrm{(R_{ct})}$ . MXene has the lowest semicircular diameter, meaning high conductivity and rapid mass transfer, as shown in Fig. 6a. Fig. 6b shows the EIS spectra for the 1st, 20th and $100\mathrm{{th}}$ cycles of the MOFs-CoP $@$ MXene, with the illustration showing the equivalent circuit diagram. The first cycle has the highest linear slope, indicating the fastest diffusion rate of lithium ions into the active material. The resistance decreased gradually with the increase of the number of cycles. In addition, GITT measure­ ment was conducted in Fig. S13. The MOFs-CoP@MXene anode has a good performance of voltage hysteresis, suggesting excellent potential in promoting ${\mathbf{{Li}}^{+}}$ reaction kinetics [48].
XPS revealed the mechanism of the synergy of MOFs-CoP $@$ MXene electrodes. In comparison to Fig. 3e, Fig. 6c shows that a new peak appears in the deconvolution of Ti 2p around $457.3\mathrm{eV}$ , which is related to $\mathrm{Ti}^{3+}$ [49]. The HR-XPS spectrum of $\mathtt{C o2p}$ shown in Fig. 6d shifts to lower binding energy compared to Fig. 3f. Peaks at 802.4 and $785.1\ \mathrm{eV}$ could be related to satellite peaks of Co. Co (II) $\mathsf{2p}_{3/2}$ and Co (II) $\mathsf{2p}_{1/2}$ were located at 781.1 and $796.7\mathrm{eV}$ [50]. Furthermore, the peaks located at $778.0$ and $793.3\ \mathrm{eV}$ were consistent with Co (III) $\mathsf{2p}_{3/2}$ and Co (III) $\mathsf{2p}_{1/2}$ [4]. There is no obvious change on the morphology of anode materials before and after cycles (Fig. S14), suggesting that the MOFsCoP $@$ MXene composite has good stability. We also plotted the reac­ tion mechanism of MOFs-CoP@MXene as the anode of LIBs, as shown in Fig. 7. The polyhedral morphology of bare MOFs-CoP is damaged because in the charge/discharge cycles, the anode materials have a large volume change. The properties of LIB anodes based on the material families, including MOFs and MXenes, are compared in Table S2. Through self-assembly with MXene substrates, MOFs-CoP is well pro­ tected by the 3D electron transport network. The MOFs-CoP@MXene has plenty of active sites, high conductivity and accelerating diffusion rate of lithium ions. This unique design of MOF-derived CoP nano­ particles and MXene nanosheets composite has excellent reversible rate performance and long-term cycle stability, which is wealthy to explore the cathode for LSBs.
The electrochemical performance of the MOFs-CoP/S and MOFsCoP@MXene/S composites as LSB cathodes is studied in Fig. 8. As mentioned earlier, according to the TGA, the sulfur content in MOFsCoP $@$ MXene/S samples is $62.7\%$ (Fig. S9). The CV curves of MOFsCoP/S and MOFs-CoP@MXene/S electrodes were tested between 1.5 and $3\ensuremath{\mathrm{V}}$ at $0.1\mathrm{mV}s^{-1}$ . The MOFs-CoP $@$ MXene/S electrode has cathodic peaks in 2.02 and $2.37~\mathrm{V}$ , as shown in Fig. 8a. The voltage range for reduction of $\ensuremath{\mathbf{\hat{s}}}_{8}$ to soluble polysulfides $(\mathrm{Li_{2}S_{x}}$ $\begin{array}{r}{\mathbf{x}=4-8\dot{}.}\end{array}$ is $1.8–2\mathrm{V}_{:}$ , and the voltage range for further reduction to small insoluble sulfides $(\mathrm{Li}_{2}S_{\mathrm{x}}$ , $\mathbf{x}=$ 1,2) is $2.25{-}2.45\mathrm{V}$ . In addition, the oxidation reaction is reflected in the anodic peak between 2.4 and $2.6\mathrm{V}$ [51]. It can be noticed that the MOFsCoP@MXene/S shifts towards the lower potential of the anodic peak and higher potential of the cathodic peak, compared to the MOFs-CoP/S. Fig. S15 shows CV curves of MOF-CoP $@$ MXene/S for the first three cycles. Obviously, the remarkable coincidence of the CV curves indicates that the MOFs-CoP $@$ MXene/S electrode has good reversibility. By comparing the first charge/discharge curve of MOFs-CoP/S, MXene/S and MOFs-CoP $@$ MXene/S at 0.2C, MOFs-CoP $@$ MXene/S exhibits the highest 1138.8 mAh $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ . Charging capacities of MOFs-CoP/S and MXene/S are 1065.6 and $807.7\ \mathrm{\mAh}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively. The MOFs$\mathsf{C o P}@$ MXene cathode has a low voltage plateau gap of $141~\mathrm{mV}$ and a low discharge overpotential of $25\mathrm{mV}$ , which facilitates the nucleation of polysulfides. Fig. 8c shows the change in the electrodes’ capacity in different cycle rates. At 6C, comparing with the capacity of MOFs-CoP/S $(445\mathrm{~mAh~g^{-1})}$ and MXene/S $(310\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ , the MOFs-CoP $@$ MXene/S electrode’s capacity remained $493\mathrm{\mAh\g^{-1}}$ . The MOFs-CoP $@$ MXene/S electrode’ capacity arrived at the high value of $854.4\ \mathrm{mAh}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , as the cycle rate reset to 0.5C. The enhanced rate capability of MOFsCoP@MXene also reflects the low voltage plateau gap and low over­ potential in Fig. 8b. Fig. 8d shows that after 300 cycles at 0.2C, the capacity of MOFs-CoP@MXene/S remained at $796.9\ \mathrm{mAh}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , much higher than that of MOFs-CoP/S $(565.1\ \mathrm{mAh}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ ). The excellent cyclic stability of MOFs-CoP $@$ MXene/S indicate that the catalytic effect of MOFs-CoP $@$ MXene on polysulfides and the low polarizability and po­ tential of MOFs-CoP $@$ MXene/S can effectively inhibit the shuttle effect through the structural engineering of MOFs-derived CoP anchored on MXene nanosheets.
To visually show the adsorption effects of different materials on LiPS, Fig. S16 shows the digital photograph depicting that the three kinds of materials were immersed in DOL/DME (v: $\mathbf{v}=1{:}1$ ) mixture containing $\mathrm{Li}_{2}S_{4}$ and $\mathrm{Li}_{2}S_{6}.$ , respectively. The $\mathrm{Li}_{2}S_{4}$ solution containing MOFsCoP@MXene is almost colorless and the $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\ensuremath{S_{6}}$ solution becomes clear after $^{12\mathrm{h}}$ . The intuitive experiment shows that MOFs-CoP $@$ MXene has a strong adsorption effect on LiPS. Fig. S17 shows the UV–vis spectroscopy of $\mathrm{MOFs-CoP@MXene/Li_{2}S_{6}}$ , MOFs- ${\mathrm{.coP//Li_{2}S_{6}}}$ , MXene/Li2S6, and $\mathrm{Li}_{2}S_{6}$ . MOFs-CoP $@$ MXene has excellent adsorption and fixation capacity for $\mathrm{Li}_{2}S_{6}$ . Fig. 9a displays the HR-XPS spectra for $\textsf{s}2\mathsf{p}$ of MOFs$\mathrm{CoP}@\mathrm{MXene}+\mathrm{Li}_{2}S_{6}$ . Peaks located at 162.0 and $163.2\ \mathrm{eV}$ can corre­ spond to $\mathsf{s}2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ and S $\mathsf{2p}_{1/2}$ , suggesting the physical adsorption of LiPS by MOFs-CoP $@$ MXene [51,52]. The long-chain polysulfide S-S bond contained in the LiPS is reflected in the peak at $164.3\mathrm{eV}$ . The formation of $S0_{4}^{2-}$ can be assigned to the peak located at $167.9\ \mathrm{eV}$ , suggesting a strong chemical reaction between LiPS and active materials. According to the XPS results, the MOFs-CoP $@$ MXene has strong adsorption and catalytic effect on LiPS, which can effectively inhibit the shuttle effect of LSBs [53]. The Nyquist plots were shown in Fig. 9b with the inset of the equivalent circuit before cycling. All Nyquist plots show two semicircles, which are named $\mathbf{R}_{\mathrm{ct1}}$ and ${\mathrm{R}}_{{\mathrm{ct}}2},$ respectively. MXene/S has the lowest $\mathrm{R_{ct1}}$ and ${\mathrm{R}}_{{\mathrm{ct}}2},$ , which are $32\Omega$ and $20\Omega$ , respectively. The introduction of MXene is beneficial to the improvement of conductivity and sulfur is well fixed by chemical bonds. MOFs-CoP $@$ MXene/S also shows both low charge transfer resistance and low contact resistance. The MOFsCoP $@$ MXene/S electrode shows good foldability (Fig. S18a). The cross-sectional view (Fig. S18b) of the electrode demonstrates the thickness of $25~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ . Fig. S18c shows that the morphology is uniform, suggesting that the 3D conductive network constructed by MOFsCoP@MXene/S has excellent uniformity. Fig. S19 shows the SEM image of cross section of MOFs-CoP $@$ MXene/S cathode and EDS map­ pings of various elements in the cathode. The sulfur element has uniform distribution. SEM images of MOFs-CoP $@$ MXene/S before and after cy­ cles were shown in Fig. S20. The surface of composite became rough, resulting in the volatile capacity to some degree. In addition, two cathodes with high sulfur loading of 3.45 and $4.12\mathrm{~mg~cm}^{-2}$ were assembled to test the electrochemical performance. As shown in Fig. S21, the capacity during 80 cycles became volatile with the increase of the sulfur loading mass. Fig. 9c shows the inherent mechanism of the inhibition of the sulfide shuttle effect by the well-designed MOFsCoP@MXene/S. Although MOFs-CoP nanoparticles have intensive interaction with polysulfides and further convert to solid $\mathrm{Li_{2}S_{2}}/\mathrm{Li_{2}S},$ , MOFs-CoP has large volume changes during cycles and could not fully contact the LiPS. MXene nanosheets provide a stable substrate for MOFsCoP and reduce the volume expansion of MOFs-CoP, thus exerting its catalytic potential. MXene nanosheets have abundant functional groups and large specific surface areas, which can capture LiPS and inhibit the shuttle effect [54]. We also disassembled CR2032 cells to confirm the inhibition mechanism of polysulfide diffusion by MOFs-CoP $@$ MXene/S. As shown in Fig. S22, the separator coated on the top of the MOFsCoP@MXene/S cathode remains transparent after 100 cycles, unlike the separator with MOFs-CoP/S becoming yellow. The introduction of MXene can improve the ability of LiPS adsorption. The properties of LSBs based on the material families, including MOFs and MXenes, are compared in Table S3. We compared the difference of water contact angle between MOFs-CoP $@$ MXene electrode used as LIB anode and LSB cathode. Figs. S23a and b show that the water contact angle on the negative side of the LIB is $14^{\circ}$ after $30~\mathsf{s}_{\mathrm{i}}$ , suggesting that the abundant hydrophilic groups on the surface of MXene can reduce the interface energy [55]. Figs. S23c and d show that after $30~\mathsf{s}_{:}$ the contact angle on the positive side of the LSB is $45^{\circ}$ , which could be caused by the loaded hydrophobic sulfur. Metal-organic frameworks-derived CoP nano­ particles are anchored on MXene nanosheets. This unique structural engineering can be used as the bifunctional electrode for enhanced lithium storage.
## 4. Conclusions
In summary, a new type of MOFs-CoP $@$ MXene has been successfully manufactured through the self-assembly method. MOFs-CoP@MXene has many advantages. On the one hand, MOFs-CoP has a unique poly­ hedral structure and defects, which has great potential in improving adsorption capacity and redox catalytic activity. On the other hand, MXene has an excellent graphene-like structure and favorable structural stability and forms a high-conductivity 3D network with active materials and shows excellent pseudocapacitive properties. As the anode of LIBs, MOFs-CoP $@$ MXene has enhanced long-term cycle stability and specific capacity. As the cathode of LSBs, MOFs-CoP $@$ MXene achieves stable sulfur electrochemical performance and effective suppression on shuttle effects. The structural engineering of MOFs-derived CoP anchored on MXene can be used as an efficient bifunctional electrode to enhance the lithium storage. This work provides an essential strategy for preparing the composites, including MOFs and MXenes, which can shed some light on energy storage.
